  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Model Identification**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the code and noticed a specific assertion on line 243. It checks if the `modelId` in the `GPT6_ASTRA_CLOUD_CONFIG` is either exactly `\'gpt-6-astra\'` or starts with `\'gpt-6\'`. Since the current value is literal, I\'m now exploring the implications of this rigid check and what other possibilities the code might have accounted for.\n\n\n**Refining Narrowing Logic**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed a TypeScript narrowing issue. The original check led to `GPT6_ASTRA_CLOUD_CONFIG.modelId` being narrowed to `never`! This makes the `startsWith` method unavailable. Replacing the problematic logic with either a direct equality check `===` or type-casting to `string` will work. The equality check, specifically, is a clean, precise solution.\n\n\n\n**Investigating Type Narrowing**\n\nI\'m now diving into a TypeScript narrowing problem. I\'ve identified that the type guard `!updateRes.ok` isn\'t correctly narrowing the `updateRes` type in this scenario. This leads to the TS2339 error, \"Property \'error\' does not exist.\" I\'ve tried type assertion and `in` checks. The underlying cause seems unrelated to `tsconfig.json`, where target is ES2022 and `strict` is absent, and using TS 5.8.2. Adding `updateRes as { ok: false; error: string }` or `if (\'error\' in updateRes)` resolves the immediate issue.\n\n\n**Examining Permission Scopes**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the skills service and permissions. Specifically, I\'m reviewing the `FloatSkillPermission` type and its usage within the `FloatSkill` interface. I\'m noting the `permissionsRequired` array\'s different values, particularly the use of type assertions like `[\'read\'] as FloatSkillPermission[]`. I\'m also examining the scope property, noting the possible values of \'built-in\', \'project\', or \'user\'. I\'ve also briefly looked at agent file and symbol indexing.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_1931632"
      name: "replace_file_content"
