  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Firebase Configuration**\n\nI\'ve been examining the Firebase configuration, specifically focusing on the project ID (\"chrome-index-0knl3\") and the Firestore database ID (\"ai-studio-float-2283c702-aaaf-4dae-a584-59f0401da1ae\"). I\'ve also noted that the user\'s Firestore database is identified as \"ai-studio-float-2283c702-aaaf.\" This data is essential to identify the correct resources in the database.\n\n\n**Investigating Cancelation Errors**\n\nI\'m now diving into the \"cancelation\" error. It seems this specific error, shaped as a raw object rejection like `{\"type\":\"cancelation\",\"msg\":\"operation is manually canceled\"}`, occurs when an operation is cancelled in AI Studio or the iframe test runner, or even due to Firestore/gRPC interactions. Further, it seems the source of the error lies within `firestore`, `@grpc/grpc-js`, `@firebase/webchannel-wrapper`, or the Firestore SDK itself, often triggered by stream closures or cancellations. I\'m focusing on tracing the exact cancellation points.\n\n\n**Refining Error Handling**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the `index.html` file, specifically lines 37-50, where I found the `unhandledrejection` event listener. I see that the `cancelation` error is caught, but `event.stopPropagation()` is used, which won\'t prevent other window listeners from triggering. I believe the solution lies in replacing it with `event.stopImmediatePropagation()` to halt propagation effectively.\n\n\n**Examining Error Propagation**\n\nI\'m now investigating error propagation in the standard browser DOM, specifically how `unhandledrejection` events behave. I\'m noting that if AI Studio or the parent container registers an `unhandledrejection` listener, failing to prevent the event or call `stopImmediatePropagation()` will cause the parent listener to still fire, and also that `window.onerror` is not a handler here. I also noticed the `window.addEventListener(\'error\', ...)` is added in `src/main.tsx` which loads as an ES module. This means any initializations before the `main.tsx` executes will be handled in a special way.\n\n\n\n**Refining Error Handling**\n\nI\'ve discovered that `index.html` exclusively uses `unhandledrejection`, *not* `error`. Further investigation of `index.html` reveals the absence of `event.stopImmediatePropagation()` within the `unhandledrejection` handler. Neither `window.onunhandledrejection` nor `window.onerror` are intercepted. I am also investigating how `reason` or `error` is checked within `unhandledrejection`, especially in cases where `event.reason` might be a cancellation object like `{ type: \'cancelation\', msg: \'operation is manually canceled\' }`. I am also seeing that if `event.stopImmediatePropagation()` is called in capture phase (`useCapture: true`), it stops ALL other listeners on `window` from ever being called.\n\n\n"
