  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Initialization Issues**\n\nI\'ve been examining the initialization process within `@monaco-editor/loader`, specifically the relationship between `loader.init()` and the `wrapperPromise`. I\'m focusing on how React 19\'s `StrictMode` interacts with this, particularly during component mounting and cleanup phases, like `useMonaco()` and `<Editor />`. I\'m starting to see potential timing issues.\n\n\n**Investigating Rejection Handling**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical issue: the `useMount` hook in `@monaco-editor/react` lacks a `.catch()` handler for the `cancelable` promise returned by `loader.init()`. This means that when a cancellation occurs, the resulting rejection, containing `{ type: \"cancelation\", msg: \"operation is manually canceled\" }`, triggers an `unhandledrejection` event in the browser. I am now looking into how AI Studio captures these errors.\n\n\n**Fixing Propagation Problems**\n\nI\'ve realized the problem isn\'t just the lack of a `.catch()` in the React hook, but also incomplete event handling. The `index.html` file in the AI Studio environment was missing `stopImmediatePropagation()`. Although I was preventing the event from bubbling up the DOM tree, other listeners, like the one injected by the host environment, were still capturing the error. I am now looking at where `stopImmediatePropagation()` needs to be added, to ensure the cancellation errors don\'t trigger the unhandled rejection.\n\n\n**Examining Error Prevention**\n\nI\'m now shifting focus to preventing the unhandled rejection entirely, rather than just handling it. I realize that `index.html`\'s missing `stopImmediatePropagation()` allows other `window` listeners to execute, even after the initial prevention. I\'m exploring pre-initializing `loader.init()` in `src/main.tsx` or wrapping `loader.init()` to proactively avoid the cancellation rejection, or by calling `loader.init().catch(() => {})`. This seems like the most robust solution. I\'m looking for a way to modify the loader module itself.\n\n\n**Implementing Rejection Prevention**\n\nI\'ve realized the most effective solution involves modifying how the promise returned by `loader.init()` handles rejections. My focus is now on ensuring that any cancellation errors are caught internally. I\'m exploring patching `loader.init()` to include a `.catch()` handler that specifically addresses cancellation errors. By adding this, I can prevent the `unhandledrejection` event from occurring in the browser, which is much more robust than the previous strategies.\n\n\n**Verifying Cancellation Sources**\n\nI\'m now tracing all potential sources of the cancellation error. I am confident the error\'s origination cannot be outside of my control, because every relevant promise is caught. To be thorough, I\'m checking if `@monaco-editor/loader` is imported directly. I\'ve confirmed that `loader` is accessible, and the package itself exists within `node_modules`. I am also checking other possible sources of the cancellation, in several files, to ensure no other listeners are left unhandled.\n\n\n**Questioning Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now questioning the error handling in `handleFirestoreError`. It seems to re-throw the cancellation error, `cancelErr`, even when a component unmounts and cancels a Firestore or gRPC stream. This re-throwing appears redundant and could be contributing to the `unhandledrejection` issue, particularly if not caught higher up. I\'m now looking at how to refine this to prevent unnecessary re-throws.\n\n\n"
